  "can_do": [
    "I can use <i>a</i> and <i>an</i> with the right sound, and make nouns plural with <i>-s, -es</i> and <i>-ies</i>.",
    "I can name irregular plurals I know, like <i>children, people</i> and <i>feet</i>.",
    "I can use <i>this, that, these</i> and <i>those</i> to talk about things near me and far away."
  ],
  "summary": [
    "Use <b>a</b> before a consonant sound and <b>an</b> before a vowel sound — listen, don't just look at the letter.",
    "Most nouns add <b>-s</b> for the plural; words ending in <b>-s, -sh, -ch, -x</b> add <b>-es</b>; consonant + <b>-y</b> changes to <b>-ies</b>.",
    "Some plurals are irregular: <i>child → children, person → people, foot → feet, mouse → mice</i>.",
    "<b>This/that</b> = one thing; <b>these/those</b> = more than one thing.",
    "<b>This/these</b> = near you; <b>that/those</b> = far away."
  ]
}
